docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:12:| **C2 — Co-founder** | **9** — Persistent judgment and temporary specialists support whole-company scope. | **9** — Standing Orders turn repeated founder decisions into delegation. | **8** — Question Trees and title forging adapt, within shared framing biases. | **8** — Parallel incarnations accelerate work; bake-offs and reconciliation add delay. | **6** — Deputies help availability; shared judgment and external-effect recovery remain fragile. | **8** — Wagers compound experience; historical agreement can preserve mistakes. | **48** |
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:14:| **C4 — Lab** | **9** — Venture and organisational experiments preserve ambition; long commitments fit less naturally. | **8** — Evidence improves decision altitude; numerous bets and appeals threaten attention. | **9** — Uncertainty maps find consequential questions and expose necessary judgment calls. | **7** — Cheap tests accelerate rejection; scarce traffic and delayed outcomes constrain validation. | **7** — Interference controls protect experiments; durable operations and outages need fuller design. | **9** — Preregistration, null results and controlled comparisons provide the strongest learning discipline. | **49** |
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:25:**C2 — strongest: judgment compiled into Standing Orders.** Repeated decisions become delegation; separate founder/own preferences preserve dissent.
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:27:**Dangerous flaw: institutionalised error.** Incarnations share one Mind; an 85% historical-agreement gate can reject improvement. Preserve constraints, not mistakes. Its A4 example also requires routine refund approval: a founder bottleneck at volume.
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:33:**C4 — strongest: preregistered experiments settled against authoritative outcomes.** Fixed criteria, preserved null results and controlled organisational trials make learning falsifiable.
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:35:**Dangerous flaw: an evidence timetable mistaken for business reality.** Delayed retention can kill sound theses prematurely. Distinguish disproved, underpowered and not-yet-observable bets. Expiry must preserve customer obligations.
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:39:**Dangerous flaw: production templates becoming the core playbook.** Fixed crews and commercial exits impose assumptions; research should not require paying users. A completion guarantor cannot silently redefine the founder’s outcome.
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:48:| **C2: continuity** | Venture Mind, wagers, founder/own-view split, Standing Orders | An on-demand strategic seat owns the thesis and allocates its authorised mission budget. |
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:53:The strategic seat funds missions; workers claim contributions. Acceptance precedes effects; receipts update state; later outcomes settle predictions. Tested lessons update skills and Standing Orders. The founder receives capital choices, intent changes and authority exceptions.
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:58:2. **C1 auction versus C4 allocator versus C3 admission.** One ledger reserves obligations and recovery, then funds hypotheses. Auditions select approaches inside funded missions. Capacity cannot be spent twice.
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:60:4. **C2 fingerprint versus C4 improvement.** Preserve constraints; let outcomes overturn historical choices. Test continuity and prospective performance.
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:71:1. **Correlated-failure budgeting.** Map ventures to shared models, skills, gateways, suppliers and demand channels. Stress simultaneous failures and limit aggregate exposure. Separate venture caps cannot contain one defective skill deployed everywhere.
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:73:3. **Complementary-investment funding.** Evaluate bundles whose parts have little standalone value: instrumentation, distribution and an offer, for example. Fund dependency milestones against the bundle’s outcome. Isolated contract and experiment rankings can reject every necessary prerequisite.
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/J2-judge-codex.md:86:- **All five: autonomy and improvement remain hypotheses.** R0-E §§2–3 supports bounded skill transfer and external feedback, not indefinite organisational improvement or simulated purchasing validity. C3/C4 preserve these distinctions most explicitly. R0-D does not establish durable profitability with negligible intervention; illustrative days and founder-minute totals remain design targets.
docs/vision-v3/r1-concepts/C3-swarm-codex.md:5:**Organising principle: change the shared environment so the next useful action becomes discoverable.** Each venture has a living world model containing goals, customers, evidence, obligations, uncertainties and unfinished work. Agents observe it, claim bounded contributions, leave verified artifacts and dissolve. Their traces attract complementary expertise, expose contradictions and inhibit unsafe actions. No agent assigns everyone’s work, owns the master plan or adjudicates every disagreement. The founder defines purpose and delegated authority; a deterministic protocol enforces that authority. Coordination intelligence lives in the workers and their shared environment. This is a proposed architecture, not a claim that autonomous, profitable swarms are already demonstrated. All numerical settings below are initial design parameters to test.
