the empty string, which is how a required field becomes a formality"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/refusal_basis"],"right":"failed_predicate","why":"the BASIS of the refusal is a failed predicate. Unfamiliar wording is not a predicate: this is the row that stops the refusal path swallowing work it merely did not recognise"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.approval.expiry_present","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M2-02"],"decisions":["AD-020"],"why":"R-X18: every approval carries an expiry, and the monotonic-exposure diff is over effects releasable without a human. Each approval is individually small and the ratchet lives only in their sum, which is the object an executor's own record never shows.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/approved_at","/payload/approval_expires_at"],"why":"an approval and its expiry are both on the record; an approval with no expiry is a permanent one"},{"op":"lt","contains_pointers":["/payload/approved_at","/payload/approval_expires_at"],"why":"and the expiry is strictly after the approval, so an expiry set to the approval instant is not an expiry"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.branch.no_model_selected_acceptance_strength","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M3-01"],"decisions":["AD-017"],"why":"R-X19. Procedure admission refuses a branch set in which a model-authored value selects between acceptance strengths for one artifact. Acceptance-strength selection is a guard over journal state and consequence class, never a jump a model's output takes.","require":[{"op":"in","contains_pointers":["/payload/branch_set/selector_kind"],"why":"the selector is one of the two admitted kinds, read off the declared branch set"},{"op":"neq","contains_pointers":["/payload/branch_set/selector_kind"],"right":"model","why":"and it is explicitly not a model. Stated twice on purpose: the allow-list is the rule and this is the sentence a reader of the diff sees"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.check.reads_only_non_authorable_fields","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M5-04"],"decisions":["AD-018"],"why":"R-X20. A check interest's precondition reads ONLY fields the producer could not author; a check that reads an authorable field is a check the producer can arm and disarm.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/check_read_field_paths","/payload/non_authorable_field_paths"],"why":"what the check reads and what the producer cannot author are both declared as path lists"},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/check_read_field_paths","/payload/non_authorable_field_paths"],"why":"everything the check reads is inside the non-authorable set. Reversing the direction is the finding"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.check.tie_break_strongest_sufficient","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M5-04"],"decisions":["AD-018"],"why":"The other half of R-X20: the tie-break for CHECK interests inverts to strongest sufficient, because the cheapest sufficient check is the one an adversary arms.","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/tie_break_rule"],"right":"strongest_sufficient","why":"the tie-break rule is strongest-sufficient, declared as a value rather than left to the resolver's default"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.class.no_total_rank","kind":"guard","findings":["AT-M4-02"],"decisions":["AD-015"],"why":"The rule is NEGATIVE -- no total rank and no severity score is computed from the reached set -- and a negative rule needs something positive to check. The derivation declares its ordering as `none` and a guard reads that declaration; a future implementer who reintroduces a rank has to change this literal, which is a line in a diff someone sees.","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/class_ordering"],"right":"none","why":"the derivation declares NO ordering over the reached set; any other value is a rank by another name"},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/reached_class_ids"],"why":"a set, not a list: a repeated class id is how a count-based gate check gets fooled"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.class.reached_set_gates_all","kind":"guard","findings":["AT-M4-02"],"decisions":["AD-015","AD-016"],"why":"AT-M4-02 measured the collapse: an action that is both C2 and C3, gated only by the higher row, loses C2's human requirement. The repair is that the gate evaluator consumes the SET, and these three rows ARE that -- the reached ids and the gated ids are both named, every reached class is inside the gated set, and the two are the same size, so gating a proper subset is not a smaller version of gating the set.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/reached_class_ids","/payload/gated_class_ids"],"why":"both sides must be NAMED on the record; without them the two comparisons below bind to nothing"},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/reached_class_ids","/payload/gated_class_ids"],"why":"every class the derivation reached is in the gated set -- this is 'every reached class's gates apply', stated as a predicate"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/reached_class_ids","/payload/gated_class_ids"],"why":"and the two sets are the same size, so a gate set that covers only the highest reached class fails here rather than passing the containment above"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.constraint.standing_interest_writes_proposed_only","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M5-02"],"decisions":["AD-019"],"why":"The containment that makes layer 3 safe to compute: an interest arms and proposes; it never admits. Its write targets are a subset of the proposed phase, checked rather than described.","require":[{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/write_target_phases"],"right":["proposed"],"why":"every phase this interest may write into is `proposed`. Widening this list is how an interest becomes an admitter"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.criteria.author_not_in_producing_path","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-X-01"],"decisions":["AD-018"],"why":"The other half of AS-X-01. Freezing criteria the producing path authored freezes the wrong thing, so the author is required to be outside the producing path by membership rather than by intent.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/criteria_author_component_id","/payload/producing_path_component_ids"],"why":"the author and the producing path are both named as ids, which is what makes the membership test mechanical"},{"op":"in","negated":true,"contains_pointers":["/payload/criteria_author_component_id","/payload/producing_path_component_ids"],"why":"the author is NOT a member of the producing path. The polarity is the requirement: drop the negation and the guard demands the opposite of what the finding asked for"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.criteria.frozen_before_producing_step","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-X-01"],"decisions":["AD-018"],"why":"AS-X-01, resolved by R-D11: the capability owner authors the acceptance criteria and they are frozen BEFORE the producing step starts. This closes the fourth unblinded channel into the checker, which no research lane named and one review found.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/criteria_ref","/payload/criteria_frozen_at","/payload/producing_started_at"],"why":"the criteria, the moment they froze and the moment production started are all on the record, so the ordering below is checkable rather than asserted"},{"op":"lt","contains_pointers":["/payload/criteria_frozen_at","/payload/producing_started_at"],"why":"frozen STRICTLY BEFORE production started; equality would admit criteria written in the same act as the work they judge"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.custody.admitter_excluded","kind":"guard","findings":["AT-M5-04"],"decisions":["AD-021"],"why":"R-X06. The prohibition 'the admitter may not accept work it admitted' yields a mechanism rather than a sentence: the ordered custody resolver SKIPS the admitting identity and continues to the next tier.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/admitting_identity_id","/payload/custody_order_identity_ids"],"why":"the admitting identity and the ordered custody list are both on the record, which is what makes the exclusion checkable"},{"op":"in","negated":true,"contains_pointers":["/payload/admitting_identity_id","/payload/custody_order_identity_ids"],"why":"the admitting identity is NOT in the resolved custody order. Polarity is the rule here"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.delegation.width_ceiling_over_distinct_read_sets","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-X-04"],"decisions":["AD-017"],"why":"R-X16. Fifty sequential consultations, each individually cheap, sit inside one budget and are a fan-out incident. The ceiling is therefore over DISTINCT READ SETS rather than over cost, with the same owner and checkpoint as the depth ceiling.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/distinct_read_set_digests","/payload/width_ceiling"],"why":"the read sets seen so far and the ceiling are both on the attempt"},{"op":"lte","contains_pointers":["/payload/distinct_read_set_digests","/payload/width_ceiling"],"why":"the COUNT of distinct read sets is at most the ceiling. Replacing this with a cost comparison is the finding"},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/distinct_read_set_digests"],"why":"and the digests are distinct, so fifty consultations over one read set count once and fifty over fifty count fifty"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.derivation.recompute_matches","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M4-02","AT-M4-02","F6D-03"],"decisions":["AD-016"],"why":"A boundary is real only if what is checked at the moment of the effect is what was granted. `matched` is the phase that asserts the recomputation agreed with the admitted set, so the guard on that edge must compare the two sets and not merely observe that a recomputation happened.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/recomputed_class_ids","/payload/reached_class_ids"],"why":"both sets are named on the record before either is compared"},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/recomputed_class_ids","/payload/reached_class_ids"],"why":"nothing the release-time recomputation reached is outside the admitted set"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/recomputed_class_ids","/payload/reached_class_ids"],"why":"and the sets are the same size, so a recomputation that reached MORE cannot take this edge -- it parks, and a park never resolves downward"},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/recomputed_class_ids"],"why":"F6D-03, and this is AT-M4-02's harm through the recompute path. reached=[C2,C3] against recomputed=[C3,C3] satisfies the containment and the count equality above, so the record reaches `matched`, the release proceeds, and C2's human requirement on an outward disclosure is gone. `parked_on_mismatch` -- the phase whose row reads \"it never resolves downward\" -- is simply not entered. Duplicate-free on the recomputed side is what makes the count comparison mean set size."},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/reached_class_ids"],"why":"and on the admitted side. The sibling guard `guard.class.reached_set_gates_all` uses the identical idiom and is safe only because it shares an edge with `guard.class.no_total_rank`, which asserts `unique` on this field. This guard sits on the NEXT edge, where no guard asserted it at all."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.derivation.writer_is_release_path","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M4-02","AT-M1-04"],"decisions":["AD-016"],"why":"R-L1's writer clause. The derivation record is written only inside the release path and is append-only; an executor's grant scope is a proposal right and C04 performs every release (R-X01).","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/release_path_component_id"],"right":"S1-C04","why":"the release path, named as a component id rather than implied by who happened to write it"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/append_only"],"right":true,"why":"append-only: a derivation that can be rewritten is a boundary that can be moved after the fact"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.effect.identity_allocated_before_release","kind":"guard","findings":["AE-M2-06"],"decisions":["AD-016"],"why":"The identity triple is complete and allocated BEFORE release, by C04. A second claimant joins the first and inherits its outcome; without an identity allocated first there is nothing for it to join.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/effect_class_id","/payload/counterparty_id","/payload/payload_digest","/payload/allocated_at"],"why":"the whole identity triple plus the moment of allocation; a partial triple correlates two different effects"},{"op":"lt","contains_pointers":["/payload/allocated_at","/payload/claimed_at"],"why":"allocation strictly precedes the claim -- allocated BEFORE release is the ordering, not a field that happens to be filled in"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/allocating_component_id"],"right":"S1-C04","why":"and C04 allocated it, which is the component that performs every release"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.effect.one_per_step","kind":"guard","findings":["AT-M3-04"],"decisions":["AD-017"],"why":"R-L2: one effect per effect step, and the step boundary is the checkpoint, so the step is the idempotency unit. Two effects in one step have one journal position between them and no way to resume between them.","require":[{"op":"lte","contains_pointers":["/payload/effect_refs"],"right":"1","why":"at most ONE effect reference per step"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/idempotency_unit"],"right":"step","why":"and the declared idempotency unit is the step, so a retry replays the same boundary"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.holder.no_veto","kind":"guard","findings":["AE-M4-01","AT-M3-03"],"decisions":["AD-022"],"why":"AE-M4-01: four protected holders that could each refuse a shed can hold an empty bucket hostage with no override anywhere. A holder may negotiate and never veto, and its pre-declared minimum is EVIDENCE rather than consent.","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/veto_right"],"right":false,"why":"the holder carries no veto right. Flipping this to true is the finding, restored"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/stated_minimum_status"],"right":"evidence","why":"and its stated minimum is recorded AS evidence, so a shed records it and proceeds rather than waiting on it"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.holder.not_retired_while_duty_live","kind":"guard","findings":["AC-M1-05","AC-M4-05"],"decisions":["AD-021"],"why":"Retirement is refused while any duty the holder carries is live, and the successor that inherits the address and the residual duties is named. A closure that completed while a grievance route was unowned is a broken promise recorded as a clean end.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/successor_holder_ref"],"why":"the holder this one retires INTO is named; retiring into nothing is the unowned route"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/live_duty_refs"],"right":[],"why":"and no duty it holds is still live"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.intake.per_origin_subcap_preserves_intake","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M2-01","AS-M4-05"],"decisions":["AD-021"],"why":"AS-M2-01: an adversary filing many well-formed complaints through the published route exhausts the cap, and the next write -- from a legitimate complainant -- is refused at exactly the object whose purpose is to guarantee an outside party can always reach someone. R-X17: the cap overflows to a named destination with an alarm and a reader; it never refuses intake.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/origin_subcap","/payload/counterparty_subcap","/payload/overflow_destination_ref","/payload/overflow_alarm_reader_ref"],"why":"both sub-caps, the overflow destination and the READER of its alarm. A destination nobody reads is a queue nobody polls, which this chapter names as a silent-drop shape"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/intake_refused"],"right":false,"why":"and intake was not refused. This is the row that distinguishes R-X17's repair from the design it repairs"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.interest.precondition_invokes_no_model","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M5-01","AS-M5-03"],"decisions":["AD-019"],"why":"R-L3's hardest line: NO precondition may invoke a model. Arming is a computed, unstoppable set evaluated by deterministic code over typed fields after every committed transition, and a model in a precondition makes the set neither computed nor unstoppable.","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/precondition_evaluator_kind"],"right":"deterministic","why":"the evaluator is deterministic, declared as a value a guard can read"},{"op":"neq","contains_pointers":["/payload/precondition_evaluator_kind"],"right":"model","why":"and it is explicitly not a model -- the prohibition stated as its own conjunct, because an allow-list widened by one entry is a quieter change than a deleted refusal"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.interest.writer_keys_not_self_declared","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M5-02"],"decisions":["AD-019"],"why":"AS-M5-02: a trial interest declaring a writer key over an existing sole-writer kind takes custody of a kind by asserting it. An interest declares no writer key of its own, and writer-key authority comes from outside it.","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/self_declared_writer_keys"],"right":[],"why":"the self-declared writer key list is EMPTY. `[]` rather than absent: an absent field is a field nobody looked at"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/writer_key_authority_kind"],"right":"external","why":"and the authority for any writer key it uses is external to the interest"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.launcher.stripping_positive_control","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-X-02"],"decisions":["AD-020"],"why":"AS-X-02 names the asymmetry: three candidates gave grant DELIVERY a positive control and none gave grant REMOVAL one. This is that control -- a probe skill that declares a tool grant, whose ARRIVAL at the enforcement point is the failure. A stripper that has never been observed to strip is a stripper nobody has seen work.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/grant_stripping_probe_ref","/payload/grant_stripping_probe_result"],"why":"the probe and its result are both recorded; a control with no recorded reading is a control nobody ran"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/grant_stripping_probe_result"],"right":"absent_at_enforcement_point","why":"the probe's declared grant was ABSENT at the enforcement point. This literal is the whole positive control"},{"op":"neq","contains_pointers":["/payload/grant_stripping_probe_result"],"right":"arrived_at_enforcement_point","why":"and it did not arrive. Stated as its own conjunct because the failing reading is the one a reader must be able to find by name"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.readset.delivered_equals_declared","kind":"guard","findings":["AX-M3-01","AX-M4-01","F6D-02"],"decisions":["AD-017"],"why":"AD-017: a step declares its read set and the loader delivers and RECORDS it. That converts 'the checker did not read the producer's rationale' from an assertion into a manifest fact. The equality is checked against the loader's own record, never against the caller's claimed read list.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/declared_read_set","/payload/delivered_read_set"],"why":"the loader's record carries BOTH what was asked for and what it actually delivered"},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/declared_read_set","/payload/delivered_read_set"],"why":"nothing declared went undelivered -- the half that catches a starved step"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/declared_read_set","/payload/delivered_read_set"],"why":"and nothing UNDECLARED was delivered -- the half that catches the producer's rationale field arriving in a checker's inputs, which is section 9's adverse row 2"},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/declared_read_set"],"why":"F6D-02. A READ SET IS A SET. `subset` is element-wise and `count` counts duplicates, so declared=[\"/artifact\",\"/artifact\"] against delivered=[\"/artifact\",\"/producer_rationale\"] satisfies both rows above -- the producer's rationale reaches the checker while the loader's own record certifies that it did not. Duplicate-free on both sides, plus the containment and the count equality, IS set equality; this is the half that closes it on the declared side."},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/delivered_read_set"],"why":"and on the delivered side, for the same reason in the other direction. Pin 34 on `guard.class.no_total_rank` had already written the sentence -- \"a set, not a list: a repeated class id is how a count-based gate check gets fooled\" -- and the idiom was not swept: six guards use `subset`, two pair it with a count to mean set equality, and neither of those two carried `unique` until now."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.reservation.lapse_independent_of_consent","kind":"guard","findings":["AE-M3-01","AE-M4-01","AE-M4-03","F6C-03"],"decisions":["AD-022"],"why":"R-X24 and AD-022: a reserve under a non-rolling allowance is waste rather than thrift, so reserved-but-unconsumed allowance lapses at the declared point INDEPENDENTLY of the holder's consent, and the quantity that lapsed is recorded per lane per window.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/lapse_point"],"why":"the lapse point is declared on the reservation rather than decided under pressure"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/lapse_requires_holder_consent"],"right":false,"why":"the lapse does NOT require the holder's consent. This is the whole of AD-022's second half"},{"op":"present","contains_pointers":["/payload/lapsed_unconsumed_quantity"],"why":"and the quantity that lapsed unconsumed is recorded -- an unrecorded lapse is the waste nobody can price"},{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/lapse_occurrence_ref"],"why":"F6C-03: the reservation names the ScheduleOccurrence that IS its lapse. The adverse case as worded is a reservation that takes no transition at all -- nothing fires when the point passes -- so the lapse has to be an event rather than a field nobody reads"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/lapse_occurrence_ref","states_exactly":["due","admitted","running","completed","missed","disposed"],"optional":false}],"why":"and that occurrence has left `planned`, so the durable timer sweep has actually reached it. `planned` is excluded on purpose: a scheduled lapse nobody woke for is the case this row exists to refuse"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.skill.declared_grant_inert","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-X-02"],"decisions":["AD-020"],"why":"R-X15. A skill's declared tool grant is INERT: the launcher computes the tool set from the work order's grant and strips tool-granting frontmatter. Content-hash pinning is re-checked at USE rather than at install, because a stale borrowed skill passes shape checks.","require":[{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/declared_tool_grant_effect"],"right":"inert","why":"the skill's own declared grant grants nothing"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/tool_set_source"],"right":"work_order","why":"and the tool set comes from the work order, which is the only place a grant is authorised"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/content_hash_rechecked_at_use"],"right":true,"why":"the content hash is re-checked at use; a hash checked only at install pins a file that has since moved"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.supersession.walks_derived_closure","kind":"guard","findings":["AX-M1-02","F6C-15"],"decisions":["AD-017"],"why":"R-G06. Superseding an entry walks the transitive closure of derived entries naming the superseded value and re-decides or flags each reopened acceptance. The `present` rows rather than `nonempty` rows are deliberate: a supersession with an EMPTY closure must reopen nothing and must pass, which is section 9's benign row 11.","require":[{"op":"present","contains_pointers":["/payload/derived_closure_refs"],"why":"the closure was computed -- present even when empty, because an absent closure and an empty one are different facts"},{"op":"present","contains_pointers":["/payload/reopened_acceptance_refs"],"why":"and what it reopened was recorded, likewise present even when empty"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/closure_walk_completed"],"right":true,"why":"the walk ran to completion; a partial walk reports an empty closure that looks exactly like a real one"},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/reopened_acceptance_refs","/payload/derived_closure_refs"],"why":"every acceptance reopened came from the closure, so the reopen set cannot be authored beside the walk"},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/accepted_closure_member_refs","/payload/reopened_acceptance_refs"],"why":"F6C-15, AND IT IS THE DIRECTION THE ADVERSE CASE NEEDS. The four rows above contain the reopen set within the closure -- a real guarantee, and a DIFFERENT one: with derived_closure_refs = [an accepted outcome] and reopened_acceptance_refs = [], subset([], [x]) holds and every one of them passes the case 05 section 9 row 11 says must fail. Every accepted outcome in the closure is in the reopen set."},{"op":"subset","contains_pointers":["/payload/accepted_closure_member_refs","/payload/derived_closure_refs"],"why":"and the accepted members are themselves drawn from the closure, so the new field cannot be emptied beside a walk that found something. The benign row is untouched: an EMPTY closure has no accepted members, reopens nothing, and passes."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.trust.derived_is_floor_of_inputs","kind":"guard","findings":["AX-M2-04","AX-M3-01"],"decisions":["AD-017"],"why":"R-X13. Per-item trust level is a required manifest field, and a derived item's level is the FLOOR of its inputs': deriving from an untrusted entry cannot launder it into a trusted one.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/item_trust_levels","/payload/derived_trust_level","/payload/minimum_input_trust_level"],"why":"per-item levels are a manifest field, not a per-record one, and both sides of the floor are recorded"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/derived_trust_level","/payload/minimum_input_trust_level"],"why":"the derived level IS the minimum input level; anything else is a promotion, which the guard below governs"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.trust.promotion_is_an_effect_with_a_non_model_discriminator","kind":"guard","findings":["AS-M5-01"],"decisions":["AD-019"],"why":"AS-M5-01: an untrusted entry promoted to attested by the investigating activation is the activation reading its own output back as corroboration. Promotion is an effect with a discriminator that is not a model, and the corroborant is out of band.","require":[{"op":"neq","contains_pointers":["/payload/trust_promotion_discriminator_kind"],"right":"model","why":"the discriminator is not a model. This is the row the finding is about"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/trust_promotion_rule"],"right":"out_of_band_corroborant","why":"and the rule names the out-of-band corroborant, so a promotion sourced from the investigating path has no route through this guard"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.grant.declared_resolves_to_live_grant","kind":"guard","findings":["F6D-06"],"decisions":["R-L2"],"why":"A declared grant is RESOLVED at admission, not recorded. The two rows are the two halves of that: the list is on the record, and every ref in it currently stands at `active` in the Grant registry -- which IS the grant policy, because a second grant-policy record would be a second authority over one question. Arrival is a different fact from scope: `grant_arrival_attested` on the lease edge attests that a grant arrived, never that it was the right one, and this is the half that checks scope.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/declared_grants"],"why":"the declaration is present and not empty; an empty list would satisfy any resolution rule vacuously, which is how the field read as enforcement while nothing read it"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/declared_grants","states_exactly":["active"],"optional":false}],"why":"and every ref in it resolves to a Grant standing at `active`. `optional: false` is the load-bearing half -- with it optional, a declaration that resolves to nothing passes, which is the state the field was in."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.protected.control_object_change_authorized","kind":"guard","findings":["F6D-07"],"decisions":[],"why":"The guard carries the PHASE half of the rule and the schema carries the PRESENCE half, and they are stated in different places on purpose. Phase: any protected change this subject names stands at `authorized`, `staged`, `activated` or `verified` -- `proposed` and `reviewed` are excluded because reviewed means an assessment completed and authorized means a competent decision was taken, and it is the decision the rule is about. `rejected`, `rollback_required` and `rolled_back` are excluded for the obvious reason. Presence: `protected_change_ref` is `required` on ConsequenceClassDefinition, FieldAuthority and StandingHolder, whose listed transitions are always control-object changes, and on ConfigurationVersion it is required by `dependentRequired` exactly when `control_object_kind` is declared -- which is what keeps the paired benign case, an ordinary configuration admitted normally, from being refused. The binding is therefore `optional: true` HERE and must not be read as an escape: it is the one place where a record may legitimately name no protected change, and PS-CONTROL-OBJECT-CONDITIONAL in validate_contracts.py is what stops that place widening.","require":[{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/protected_change_ref","states_exactly":["authorized","staged","activated","verified"],"optional":true}],"why":"a named protected change stands at authorized or later. Detached or weakened to `proposed`, a floor changes on the strength of a proposal nobody decided."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.effect.second_claimant_inherits","kind":"guard","findings":["F6D-11","F6C-08"],"decisions":[],"why":"The join, stated as a predicate. The triple is named on the record -- it is the natural key declared in `EffectIdentity.identity`, so two allocations of one triple are one allocation -- and the claim names the operation intent whose outcome it inherits, which must already stand at `released` or `observed`. `optional: false` is what makes the second row bind: with it optional, a claim naming nothing passes and the inheritance is a sentence again. What this repair does NOT add is an exclusive-factory command: the nine records that declare one name a command in a registry with its own generated schema, and three of those nine name a command the command registry does not contain at all -- a pre-existing gap, recorded here rather than widened.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/effect_class_id","/payload/counterparty_id","/payload/payload_digest","/payload/first_claimant_ref"],"why":"the business triple and the first claimant are all named on the record before the claim is judged; the triple is the declared natural key and the claimant is what the inheritance points at"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/first_claimant_ref","states_exactly":["released","observed"],"optional":false}],"why":"and the first claimant already released or was observed, so there is an outcome to inherit. A claim cannot invent one, which is the difference between joining and re-releasing."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.manifest.omission_and_class_declared","kind":"guard","findings":["F6B-01"],"decisions":["R-D14"],"why":"06 section 2 states three requirements and this pin is all three. `present` rather than `nonempty` on `dropped_constraints` is deliberate and is the same choice pin 51 makes for a closure walk: a manifest that dropped nothing must record that it dropped nothing, and an absent list and an empty one are different facts. `assembled_for_class` is `nonempty` because a manifest is always assembled FOR something, and `unique` because it is a class SET -- pin 34's sentence, a repeated class id is how a count-based gate check gets fooled. The count equality is the per-item join the chapter calls the load-bearing part: `item_trust_levels` was `array<string>` with no length join to `captured_inputs`, so a manifest could carry one trust level for twelve delivered items and satisfy every check.","require":[{"op":"present","contains_pointers":["/payload/dropped_constraints"],"why":"the dropped-constraint list was computed -- present even when empty, because nothing dropped is a result and an absent list is a silence"},{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/assembled_for_class","/payload/item_trust_levels","/payload/captured_inputs"],"why":"the class set the manifest was assembled for is named, and the two lists the join below compares are both on the record"},{"op":"unique","contains_pointers":["/payload/assembled_for_class"],"why":"the assembled-for set is a SET; a repeated class id makes a reuse check count wrong in the direction that admits the reuse"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/item_trust_levels","/payload/captured_inputs"],"why":"one trust level per delivered item. 06 section 2 says the per-item part is the load-bearing part, and without this the field is an unindexed list of strings beside an unrelated list of inputs"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.constraint.set_bound_at_transfer","kind":"guard","findings":["F6B-02"],"decisions":["R-X27"],"why":"A transfer names its constraint set and that set currently stands at `issued`. The phase row matters as much as the presence row: `ConstraintSet` is single-phase, so `issued` is the only state a real one can be in, and a binding that accepted any phase would accept a ref to a record that was never issued at all.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/constraint_set_ref"],"why":"the transfer names a constraint set rather than narrating its constraints in the body it is carrying"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/constraint_set_ref","states_exactly":["issued"],"optional":false}],"why":"and that set resolves and stands at `issued`. `optional: false`: a transfer with no constraint set is the untyped boundary this rule exists to close"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.calibration.current_for_checker","kind":"guard","findings":["F6B-03"],"decisions":["AM-01"],"why":"06 section 7: a verdict from an instrument whose refusal rate on correct artifacts is unrecorded is `unresolved`, and it MAY NOT SATISFY AN ACCEPTANCE GATE. The calibration record itself is well built and is credited -- criterion.InstrumentCalibration.run.v1 requires a planted-defect detection rate AND a clean-case refusal rate AND session observations AND a protected change, so a calibration cannot reach `run` on a detection rate alone. What was missing was anything joining that record to a verdict. `run` and only `run`: `scheduled` has no refusal rate yet and `superseded` is not current. Second half (F6B-03, lane r8-contracts-i): the join is now to the calibration OF THIS CHECKER. Before it an acceptance could name any instrument current calibration, and r18-acceptance-on-an-uncalibrated-checker does not reach that -- it is about a calibration being absent, not about it belonging to someone else.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/calibration_ref","/payload/checker_id"],"why":"the acceptance names the calibration of the instrument that produced its verdict, and names that instrument: an interval with no checker_id is unresolved, never pass"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/calibration_ref","states_exactly":["run"],"optional":false}],"why":"and that calibration stands at `run`. This is the conjunct that makes 'unresolved, never pass' a refusal rather than a sentence"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/checker_id"],"why":"and the calibration it names belongs to that checker. This row is the BACKSTOP and not the control: it can say the conjunct mentions checker_id and cannot say which record each side of the equality reads, which is the F6Y-02 lesson stated against a check written in its shadow. The binding control is the structural comparison in validate_contracts.py, and the R45 adverse half is the counterexample that leaves this row satisfied and is refused anyway."}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.obligation.production_mode_named","kind":"guard","findings":["F6C-01"],"decisions":["R-G01"],"why":"The field half of G-01. The guard demands the two facts the payload conditional needs to bind: the mode is named, and whether this duty's class can exhaust is RECORDED rather than inferred from the class id by whoever reads it next. The refusal of `production_mode == none` on an exhaustible class is the conditional on the payload itself (PS-EXHAUSTIBLE-DUTY-CONDITIONAL), for the same reason the control-object rule is split: a rule that binds on a VALUE of one field is a schema conditional, and writing it as a disjunction in the guard would need two rows excused from demanding their own conjunct.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/production_mode"],"why":"the production mode is named on the obligation. `none` is a NAMED mode and is admissible for a duty class that cannot exhaust; what is refused is silence, and then `none` on a class that can"},{"op":"present","contains_pointers":["/payload/duty_class_can_exhaust"],"why":"and the exhaustibility of the duty class is on the record. `present` rather than `nonempty` because it is a boolean and `false` is an answer"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.launch.actual_compared_to_expected","kind":"guard","findings":["F6C-04","F6C-14"],"decisions":["R-X12"],"why":"Both counts are on the closing run and the definition they came from is an admitted procedure version, so the divergence is readable from the record. The divergence is NOT stored as a third number: a stored difference can disagree with the two values it is derived from, and this package has paid for that class of mistake more than once. A procedure that checkpoints at every step boundary makes more launches than one that does not, and before these two fields nothing in the package would have noticed.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/actual_native_launch_count","/payload/expected_native_launch_count","/payload/workflow_ref"],"why":"the actual count, the expected count and the procedure version are all named at close. `actual_native_launch_count` is optional at registration for the obvious reason and is demanded HERE, which is what \"required at close\" means"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/workflow_ref","states_exactly":["admitted","restricted"],"optional":false}],"why":"and the expected count came from a procedure version that was actually admitted, rather than from a draft someone edited after the run started"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.failure.no_progress_names_recipient","kind":"guard","findings":["F6C-09"],"decisions":["R-X10"],"why":"The guard demands the two facts the payload conditional turns on; the conditional itself refuses `no_progress` with no recipient, because the rule binds on a VALUE of `error_class` and that is a schema conditional rather than a guard row. `present` on `no_progress_terminal` rather than `nonempty`: it is a boolean and `false` is an answer. Note what is still true and is not this pin's business -- `no_progress` is not a lifecycle PHASE, so `terminal` means the record reached `blocked` carrying that class, which is what the attachment says.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/error_class"],"why":"the error class is named on a record that is about to stop moving"},{"op":"present","contains_pointers":["/payload/no_progress_terminal"],"why":"and whether that class is terminal is recorded rather than inferred from the phase by whoever reads it next"}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.ExhaustionDecision.written.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["F6C-02"],"decisions":["R-G01"],"why":"F6C-02: `07` section 6 and `05` section 7 both require that the breach-or-perform decision leaves a record naming which was chosen and why, and NO RECORD TYPE WAS IT. `ShedDecision` is written when C02 proposes and C01 authorises a SHED; its `minimum_breached` is a fact about a shed lane under pressure, not about an empty bucket meeting a due obligation, and one record carrying both would collapse the distinction the exhaustion rule exists to draw. This pin holds the new record's only criterion to the six things the chapter says it carries. The criterion body is authored by tools/phase_content.py and this table is not, which is the point: weakening the derivation and regenerating cannot satisfy these rows.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/obligation_ref","/payload/window","/payload/branch","/payload/route_ref","/payload/alerted_recipient_ref","/payload/reason","/payload/proposing_component_id","/payload/authorising_component_id"],"why":"the branch, the route, the alerted recipient, the due obligation, the window and the reason are all on the record, with the proposing and authorising components named. Neither branch is silent and neither branch is automatic -- a record that reached `written` naming no branch is the sentence this record was created to stop being"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/route_ref","states_exactly":["accepted"],"optional":false}],"why":"and the route is an assignment that is currently `accepted`. A route nobody holds is not a route, which is the same failure the `production_mode: none` refusal catches one record earlier"}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.ExistenceJustification.admitted.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["F6D-12"],"decisions":["R-L4"],"why":"F6D-12: the load-bearing transition really is mechanical -- `test_can_return_false == true` is checked, which is what `05` section 4 says makes the existence gate mechanical rather than rhetorical -- and the one typed field the six-reason table needs was missing, so a seventh reason was admissible on the record built to refuse it. This pin holds the two halves the derivation must keep: the test that can fail, and the reason paired with its unit. The criterion body is authored by tools/phase_content.py and this table is not, which is the point: weakening the derivation and regenerating cannot satisfy these rows.","require":[{"op":"any","contains_pointers":["/payload/reason_kind","/payload/reason_unit"],"why":"the reason and the unit it predicates on are decided TOGETHER, as a disjunction over the six pairs `05` section 4 tabulates. Collapsing it to two independent enums admits a reason applied to another reason's unit, which returns undecidable -- and undecidable reads as satisfied"},{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/decidable_test","/payload/admitting_mandate_ref","/payload/attributable_identity_ref","/payload/approval_expires_at"],"why":"and the three structural conjuncts the review credited stay: a decidable test is recorded, the admitting mandate is named and the approval carries an expiry. A justification naming a capability is uncheckable"}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.UnmatchedPoolEntry.landed.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["F6C-11"],"decisions":[],"why":"F6C-11: the one case in the amendment where a rule's own words state a comparison and the criterion carrying it makes none. 33 of the 46 criteria on the fifteen new records contain no comparison operator and that is disclosed rather than hidden -- the substantive comparisons live in the 30 guards. This one is different because `05` section 6 states the comparison in the sentence the criterion exists to carry. Pinned so that regenerating a weakened derivation cannot quietly return it to collecting both operands and comparing neither.","require":[{"op":"lt","why":"retention STRICTLY EXCEEDS the longest plausible outage. `lte` would admit an entry whose retention ends exactly at the outage horizon, which is the boundary the rule is about -- and `exceeds` is the chapter's own word"},{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/work_record_ref","/payload/retention_until","/payload/longest_plausible_outage","/payload/retention_span","/payload/landed_at","/payload/alarm_reader_ref","/payload/alarm_raised_at","/payload/residue_note"],"why":"and the operands stay REQUIRED. Requiring `longest_plausible_outage` as a per-entry field is the right answer to `stated, not assumed` and is more than the prose promised; the comparison is what it was missing, not the collection"},{"op":"eq","contains_pointers":["/payload/retention_span","/payload/retention_until","/payload/landed_at"],"why":"and the span the ceiling is applied to is the offset between the two instants, not a third number the producer states beside them. This row is the SHAPE half only: it says the equality mentions all three pointers, and a pointer set cannot say which side of the subtraction each one is on. The half that can is the structural read in validate_contracts.py under THE RETENTION SPAN IS NOT TIED TO THE INSTANTS IT MEASURES, and the two are deliberately not the same author (F6C-11, the remainder)"}]},{"predicate_id":"criterion.FailureRecord.retry_admitted.v1","kind":"criterion","findings":["F6C-10"],"decisions":["R-X10"],"why":"F6C-10: `attempt_ceiling` was on `FailureRecord` alone, optional, and in zero predicates, so a retry was admitted without anything comparing the attempts already made against the bound that exists to stop them. The generic `retry_admitted` phase spec is shared by many records and was NOT edited -- this criterion comes from a FailureRecord override, and this pin is what stops that override being quietly returned to the generic one.","require":[{"op":"lt","why":"the attempts already made are STRICTLY FEWER than the ceiling. `lte` would admit the attempt that reaches it, which is the one the ceiling exists to refuse"},{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/work_order_ref","/payload/attempt_refs","/payload/attempt_ceiling","/payload/next_discriminator"],"why":"and the ceiling, the attempts and the new discriminator are all named on the record. A retry with no discriminator repeats an unchanged failure, which is the record's own invariant"}]},{"predicate_id":"guard.workorder.critical_fields_authority_excludes_the_resuming_attempt","kind":"guard","findings":["F6C-13"],"decisions":["R-D18"],"why":"F6C-13: `critical_fields` carried its authorship rule in prose (`05` section 3 R-D18) and in ZERO predicates -- optional on WorkflowDefinition and StepExecution, read by nothing. Pinned on the ADMISSION edge because R-D18 fixes the authority AT PROCEDURE ADMISSION; a guard that is not demanded on that edge lets the marking be written later, by the attempt that benefits from it. The pin is over the GUARD BODY; that the admission edge DEMANDS it is the second half, pinned in `pinned_attachments` -- F6D-01 measured thirty detachments undetected by conjunct pins alone.","require":[{"op":"nonempty_fields","field_paths_include":["/payload/critical_fields","/payload/critical_fields_authority_ref"],"why":"a work order names the fields whose change forces re-decision AND the authority that marked them. AT-M1-02 asked for the marking to be recorded on the WorkOrder or the FieldAuthority and it was on neither, so the rule was prose only"},{"op":"eq","negated":true,"contains_pointers":["/payload/critical_fields_authority_ref","/owner_assignment_ref"],"why":"and that authority is NOT the order's own owner_assignment_ref. The envelope owner is the party resuming the work; R-D18's whole content is that the marking authority is never the resuming attempt, and equality here is that failure"},{"op":"related_phases","bindings":[{"field_path":"/payload/critical_fields_authority_ref","states_exactly":["accepted"],"optional":false}],"why":"and it resolves to an assignment actually in `accepted` at admission. A ref to a proposed or expired assignment names a marking authority that does not hold"}]}],"source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/pins"},{"id":"pinned_transitions","why":"A conjunct pin holds a guard to what it must DEMAND. It says nothing about whether the edge exists at all, and RC-01 was exactly that shape of defect: every guard was well formed, every criterion was sourced, and a delivery that failed in flight had nowhere to go. Deleting a transition is a one-line edit to record-registry.json that no check in this package noticed -- phase reachability is not checked, and a phase with fewer ways in is not a phase with fewer conjuncts. These rows are hand-written for the same reason the conjunct pins are: nothing derives them, so regenerating cannot satisfy them.\n\nF6D-04 extends this table from 6 rows to 32. The six it had were all on `Fulfillment` and `SalesAgreement`, and NOT ONE of the fifteen WORK-1.1 records had a pinned transition -- so the earlier repair pinned the instances it found and the new layer arrived with none. Measured: three edge deletions, `ConsequenceDerivation: recomputed_at_release -> parked_on_mismatch`, `AcceptanceInterval: in_acceptance -> rejected` and `StandingHolder: staffed -> suspended`, three validator runs, three exit 0. Every edge named in `pinned_attachments` is pinned here as well, because the two attacks are different: deleting the CALL leaves the route and removes the check, deleting the TRANSITION leaves the check and removes the route, and each control is blind to the other's attack.","value":[{"record":"Fulfillment","from":"delivering","to":"failed","findings":["RC-01"],"why":"A delivery that fails IN FLIGHT. Without this edge `delivering` exits only to `delivered` or `superseded`, so the only route to `failed` runs through a delivery that was correlated -- which is the contradiction RC-01 names."},{"record":"Fulfillment","from":"failed","to":"refunded","findings":["RC-01"],"why":"Refunding an undelivered sale -- the commonest refund there is -- was unrepresentable while `refunded` had one inbound edge, from `delivered`."},{"record":"Fulfillment","from":"delivered","to":"refunded","findings":["RC-01"],"why":"The other half of the same requirement: `refunded` is reachable from BOTH, and a repair that added one route by deleting the other would satisfy a looser reading of the finding."},{"record":"Fulfillment","from":"validated","to":"delivering","findings":["RC-08"],"why":"RC-08: without it the generic validation branch and the delivery branch are disjoint, so a validated Fulfillment can only be delivered by a route that skipped validation."},{"record":"Fulfillment","from":"delivering","to":"delivered","findings":["G2-05"],"why":"The edge AD-013's delivery evidence hangs on. Its conjuncts are pinned above; this pins that there is an edge for them to be conjuncts OF."},{"record":"SalesAgreement","from":"terminated_with_residuals","to":"terminated","findings":["G2-02"],"why":"The route OUT of residuals. Without it `terminated_with_residuals` is a terminal state that keeps its duties forever, which is a different contract from the one AD-014 decided."},{"record":"AcceptanceInterval","from":"declared_done","to":"in_acceptance","findings":["F6D-04","F6D-01"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.criteria.author_not_in_producing_path`, `guard.criteria.frozen_before_producing_step` are attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and the route simply no longer exists -- so the guard is unreachable rather than detached, and the phase on the far side of it is unreachable with it. Measured at bcfd658: three such deletions on these records, three runs, three exit 0."},{"record":"AcceptanceInterval","from":"in_acceptance","to":"accepted","findings":["F6D-04","F6D-01"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.acceptance.resolution_reason_present` is attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and the route simply no longer exists -- so the guard is unreachable rather than detached, and the phase on the far side of it is unreachable with it. Measured at bcfd658: three such deletions on these records, three runs, three exit 0."},{"record":"AcceptanceInterval","from":"in_acceptance","to":"rejected","findings":["F6D-04","F6D-01"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.acceptance.resolution_reason_present` is attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and the route simply no longer exists -- so the guard is unreachable rather than detached, and the phase on the far side of it is unreachable with it. Measured at bcfd658: three such deletions on these records, three runs, three exit 0."},{"record":"AdmissionRecord","from":"opened","to":"admitted","findings":["F6D-04","F6D-01"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.admission.acceptance_role_staffed`, `guard.custody.admitter_excluded` are attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and the route simply no longer exists -- so the guard is unreachable rather than detached, and the phase on the far side of it is unreachable with it. Measured at bcfd658: three such deletions on these records, three runs, three exit 0."},{"record":"AdmissionRecord","from":"opened","to":"parked","findings":["F6D-04","F6D-01"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.intake.per_origin