ned_transitions"},{"id":"requires_waivers","why":"RC-03. `requires` and `conjuncts` are independent fields of one spec and nothing compared them, so a criterion could state one requirement and demand another -- which is what the weakened derivation produced: the sentence still promised delivery over a body that had stopped asking for it. Measured before any waiver: 774 criteria examined, 0 failing the field-path rule, 17 failing the phase-name rule, every one of the 17 a phase-kind synonym for the criterion's OWN phase. Each waiver below names the exact tokens it excuses, so a new unmatched phase on a waived criterion still fails. SCOPE, stated because the rule's name promises more than the rule does (RC2-03). What is compared is a FORMATTING-DEPENDENT SUBSET of the sentence: backticked lowercase tokens (PROSE_TOKEN) and field paths that are either backticked or written literally as /payload/... (PROSE_PATH). English nouns are not compared to conjuncts, and most of what these sentences are made of is English nouns. Removing three pairs of backticks leaves a sentence promising a reader exactly the same thing and invisible to the rule -- the recheck measured that, and the run went from stopping at RC-03 in 19 seconds to passing it and failing 640 seconds later for an unrelated reason. LIVE POPULATION, re-derived on the committed corpus with the disjunction-aware walker: 774 criteria examined; 0 of them (0.0%) name a field path in `requires`, so the PATHS rule -- the half RC-03's required contract actually named -- has an applicable population of ZERO and contributes no live assertion; 19 (2.5%) name a backticked phase of a related record, 22 tokens in all; 17 of those 19 rely on a waiver below, and all 17 waivers are in use; the remaining 5 tokens across 2 criteria (criterion.SalesAgreement.performed.v1: `delivered`, `discharged`, `transferred`; criterion.GrievanceCase.remedy_authorized.v1: `held`, `partly_consumed`) are bound by a conjunct and are the whole of the rule's live force. THE NARROW SCOPE IS THE ACCEPTED LIMIT, and the alternative was measured rather than argued. Widening the phase rule to unbackticked tokens of related records' phase vocabulary produces 425 findings across 266 of the 774 criteria (34%), over 62 distinct tokens -- case-sensitive, and already excluding every token a conjunct binds or a waiver excuses. The vocabulary is ordinary English past participles used as verbs: `recorded` 104 (\"the subject's own declared identity/scope fields are recorded\"), `required` 43 (\"no judgment is required\"), `current` 37, `admitted` 34, `assigned` 30 (\"a custodian is assigned\"). A waiver list of that size is not a waiver list, it is a second corpus, and a rule that demands one teaches contributors to route around the checker. So the rule keeps its scope, the number is written down here rather than left to be rediscovered, and what is added instead is the control the live half never had: fixtures/negative/r14-requires-names-a-related-phase-no-conjunct-binds. Until it, the only RC-03 fixture exercised the half with zero population. The cure for the formatting dependency is upstream and is not a checker change. A criterion's `requires` sentence and its conjuncts are written by one author in one entry of tools/phase_content.py, so what makes the rule reach is the author backticking the phases the sentence promises. That is a CONVENTION, nothing enforces it, and this sentence exists so that nobody reads the rule's name as though something did.","value":[{"criterion":"criterion.AgentTemplate.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while AgentTemplate spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.BuildArtifact.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while BuildArtifact spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.CasePool.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while CasePool spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.ConfigurationVersion.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while ConfigurationVersion spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make 